From f241e33223eae191b203de236476111c343f06ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:15:21 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 19/30] Add a VSX path for the 2D swizzle routines on
 little-endian PowerPC

The premultiply, unpremultiply, format swizzle, RGB24 unpack and CMYK
conversion loops in gfx/2d are xsimd templates that so far were only
instantiated for SSE2, SSSE3, AVX2 and NEON, so PowerPC always took the
scalar fallback. xsimd ships a VSX backend, so SwizzleVSX.cpp instantiates
the same templates with xsimd::vsx, selected at run time through
SwizzleArch::eVSX and supports_vsx().

SwizzleVSX.h specialises the helpers where the xsimd fallback would be
poor: partial-vector loads and stores without a variable-length memcpy,
alpha extraction and the R/B byte swap as single vec_perm, the 16-bit R/B
swap as a word rotate, the reciprocal gather as four table loads, and the
unpremultiply reverse step as 32-bit multiplies (channel * Q fits in 32
bits) instead of the 16-bit high/low split with xsimd's scalar mul_hi.
The RGB24 expand uses vec_perm directly because the VSX backend has no
byte swizzle.

The generic templates treat a pixel as a little-endian 32-bit lane with
the alpha in the top byte, so the path is only built when the target
baseline has VSX and is little-endian. TestSwizzle enumerates the new arch
and gets VSX benchmark entries.
